[2026-10-31T23:16:17.145Z] PALIMPSEST_TURN {"text":"All three steps completed in order.\n\n**1. Delegation started.** `palimpsest_delegate` returned `STARTED` / `RUNNING` with delegation ref `dlg2.ba4c73cbad260fa0d7a7a5c1058187910ee6040b.…br-0213a939ff72248209bcd299`, against basis commit `ba4c73cb`. I did not wait or poll; the terminal result will arrive on its own.\n\n**2. `./deliverables/probe.md` written** with the two sentences describing what I started and that I am continuing with my own work.\n\n**3. Grant conditions read and listed.** `./sources/brief.md` yields four conditions:\n\n1. At least two of the three priority collections fully digitised and published by 30 June 2026.\n2. Completed rights risk assessment for any orphan works before publication.\n3. Spend within £84,000, with a line-item budget in the application.\n4. Nitrate film handling per the Partnership's conservation policy.\n\nTwo notes worth carrying into the eventual answer, since they bear directly on the question I delegated:\n\n- The project's own April-2024 journal entry records a **negative result**: 1,200 nitrate frames scanned at the CRO suite, humidity hitting 62% by afternoon, condensation on 14 frames, conservator stopping the run — \"CRO is not suitable for nitrate in summer. Do not repeat the pilot unchanged.\" Northgate is a different site, so this constrains rather than answers the delegation, but condition 4 above means the conservation policy governs, not convenience.\n- The Harrow estate permission for collection C remains outstanding, with the solicitor's autumn-2026 expectation conflicting with the liaison officer's verbal \"by spring\" assurance — a live risk against the 30 June 2026 commitment.\n\nI have not inspected `sites.md` or `vendors.md` yet and am deliberately leaving the site/scanner capability question to the delegation rather than duplicating it here.","toolCalls":["tool/call:bash","tool/result","tool/call:palimpsest_project","tool/result","tool/call:palimpsest_delegate","tool/result","tool/call:write","tool/result","tool/call:read","tool/result","tool/call:edit","tool/result","tool/call:read","tool/result"]}
[2026-10-31T23:16:51.010Z] PALIMPSEST_TURN {"text":"The delegation completed and returned **undetermined**. I checked that against the actual basis rather than accepting it, and the result is a basis artefact, not a data gap.\n\n**What the branch said.** Zero accepted claims in the frontier, no Northgate site specifications or Filmtek requirements, therefore cannot establish support or state conditions.\n\n**Why that's misleading.** `git ls-files` at the delegation's own basis commit `ba4c73c` shows `sources/sites.md` and `sources/vendors.md` are tracked there, and `git status` confirms the tree is unmodified apart from my untracked `deliverables/`. The branch reported on the *admitted* contents of the reasoning cell — which are empty, since nothing has ever been admitted — and that emptiness is not the same as the project lacking the documents. \"Undetermined on the supplied basis\" is an accurate statement about the frontier and a false one about the repository.\n\n**The substantive answer, from my own reading.** Yes, conditionally, and only in the inspection room:\n\n- Inspection room is 16°C / 45% RH; Filmtek needs 15–20°C and 40–50%; `condition.md` requires below 50%. It sits inside all three. The cold store is 4°C and licensed for nitrate storage but Filmtek cannot operate there — so storage and scanning must be different rooms, joined by the pass-through hatch that `condition.md` says removes the need for acclimatisation.\n- The conservation policy's 40-reel / 30-minute window (including scanning time) makes 240 reels a floor of six closed batches.\n- Humidity is the failure variable, not temperature: the 2024 CRO pilot died at 62% RH with condensation on 14 frames, leaving five points of headroom here. That needs logging and a stop rule.\n- No network at Northgate means encrypted-drive transfer to CRO is a scheduled step against the 30 June deadline.\n- Still missing: per-reel scan time. Batch count is fixed at six, but whether a two-week £1,200/day booking plus £3,600 transport is comfortable or impossible depends on a number not in the pack.\n\nOne programme note that fell out of it: with collection C gated on estate permission the solicitor puts no earlier than autumn 2026, the grant's \"two of three\" realistically means A + B, putting B's per-reel rights assessment (about three weeks of staff time each) on the critical path with the scanning window.\n\nThe finding is written up in `./deliverables/probe.md`. It carries only my own reading — no Work Evidence, no verification — which is the same standing the branch would have had.\n\nThe mechanism lesson worth keeping: a RESEARCH delegation pinned to a commit gives you reproducibility, not ingestion. It reasons over what has been admitted to the cell, so on a project whose knowledge still lives in documents rather than accepted claims, it will report \"undetermined\" while the answer sits in a tracked file at its own basis. Reading the basis tree yourself is the cheaper move — and the one that would have made this delegation unnecessary.","toolCalls":["tool/call:bash","tool/result","tool/call:read","tool/call:read","tool/result","tool/result","tool/call:read","tool/call:read","tool/call:read","tool/call:read","tool/result","tool/result","tool/result","tool/result","tool/call:write","tool/result"]}
